# Summary of the Bill (Starting at Sec. 70401)

## Subchapter A — Investments in Families and Children

**Sec. 70401. Enhancement of employer-provided child care credit.**
This section increases the employer-provided child care credit (IRC §45F) so that 40% of qualified child care expenditures count toward the credit (up from 25%), or 50% for an "eligible small business" (one meeting the §448(c) gross receipts test using a 5-year lookback). The maximum annual credit rises from $150,000 to $500,000 ($600,000 for eligible small businesses), with inflation adjustments after 2026. It also allows credit claims for payments made through third-party intermediaries contracting with qualified child care facilities, and clarifies that jointly owned/operated facilities qualify. These changes apply to amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70402. Enhancement of adoption credit.**
This makes up to $5,000 of the adoption credit refundable (payable even if the taxpayer owes no tax), with the $5,000 amount inflation-adjusted beginning after 2024. The nonrefundable portion remains subject to existing carryforward rules, but the refundable portion is excluded from the 5-year carryforward calculation. All dollar amounts in the adoption credit (maximum credit, income phase-outs) are now indexed to inflation using a 2001 base year. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70403. Recognizing Indian tribal governments for purposes of determining whether a child has special needs for purposes of the adoption credit.**
This amends IRC §23(d)(3) so that a determination by an Indian tribal government—rather than only a State—that a child cannot or should not be returned to their parents qualifies the child as having "special needs" for the full adoption credit. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70404. Enhancement of the dependent care assistance program.**
This raises the tax-free dependent care assistance limit under IRC §129 from $5,000 ($2,500 for married individuals filing separately) to $7,500 ($3,750 for married filing separately). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70405. Enhancement of child and dependent care tax credit.**
This restructures the child and dependent care credit (IRC §21) to start at a 50% applicable percentage, reduced by 1 percentage point for each $2,000 of adjusted gross income above $15,000 (down to a floor of 35%), and further reduced by 1 percentage point for each $2,000 ($4,000 joint) above $75,000 ($150,000 joint), with a floor of 20%. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

## Subchapter B — Investments in Students and Reforms to Tax-Exempt Institutions

**Sec. 70411. Tax credit for contributions of individuals to scholarship granting organizations.**
This creates a new federal income tax credit (IRC §25F) of up to $1,700 per year for individuals who donate cash to scholarship granting organizations (501(c)(3) non-private foundations) that fund K-12 private school scholarships for students from households earning up to 300% of area median gross income. States must voluntarily opt in, with the Governor (or designated official) submitting an annual list of qualifying organizations to the Treasury Secretary by January 1 each year. Participating organizations must serve at least 10 students across multiple schools, spend at least 90% of income on scholarships, prioritize prior-year recipients and siblings, and cannot earmark donations for specific students or award scholarships to "disqualified persons." Unused credits carry forward up to 5 years, the credit is reduced by any state credit claimed for the same donation, and a companion provision (§139K) excludes scholarship amounts from recipients' gross income; both apply to taxable years ending after December 31, 2026.

**Sec. 70412. Exclusion for employer payments of student loans.**
This makes permanent the exclusion from gross income for up to $5,250 per year of employer-paid student loan assistance (which was previously scheduled to expire for payments after January 1, 2026). The $5,250 amount is indexed to inflation for taxable years beginning after 2026, rounded to the nearest $50. Effective for payments made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70413. Additional expenses treated as qualified higher education expenses for purposes of 529 accounts.**
This expands the list of K-12 expenses payable tax-free from 529 accounts to include curriculum materials, books, online educational materials, tutoring (by licensed teachers, experienced instructors, or subject-matter experts not related to the student), standardized/advanced placement/college admission test fees, dual-enrollment fees, and educational therapies for students with disabilities. The annual K-12 distribution limit doubles from $10,000 to $20,000. The expanded expense list applies to distributions made after enactment; the $20,000 limit applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70414. Certain postsecondary credentialing expenses treated as qualified higher education expenses for purposes of 529 accounts.**
This allows 529 funds to be used tax-free for "qualified postsecondary credentialing expenses"—tuition, fees, books, supplies, required testing fees, and continuing education for recognized postsecondary credential programs. Qualifying credentials include industry-recognized certifications (accredited by bodies like the Institute for Credentialing Excellence or listed in Defense Department COOL directories), registered apprenticeship completion certificates, and occupational/professional licenses issued by state or federal governments. Programs must appear on a State Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act list, the VA's WEAMS directory, or be recognized by the Treasury Secretary in consultation with the Secretary of Labor. Effective for distributions made after enactment.

**Sec. 70415. Modification of excise tax on investment income of certain private colleges and universities.**
This replaces the flat 1.4% endowment excise tax on private colleges with a tiered structure: 1.4% for institutions with a student adjusted endowment of $500,000–$750,000, 4% for $750,000–$2,000,000, and 8% above $2,000,000. The threshold for coverage rises to institutions with at least 3,000 tuition-paying students (up from 500), with more than 50% of tuition-paying students located in the U.S., and a student adjusted endowment of at least $500,000. The tax base is expanded to count student loan interest income and "Federally-subsidized royalty income" (royalties from intellectual property developed with any Federal funds) as net investment income, and assets/income of related organizations are attributed to the institution. Institutions must report tuition-paying student counts on their returns; effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70416. Expanding application of tax on excess compensation within tax-exempt organizations.**
This broadens the definition of "covered employee" for the 21% excise tax on excess compensation (IRC §4960) to include all former employees of applicable tax-exempt organizations who were employees in any taxable year beginning after December 31, 2016—previously the tax applied only to the five highest-paid employees. This means golden parachute payments and post-employment compensation to any former employee above the $1 million threshold are now subject to the tax. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

## Subchapter C — Investments in Community Development

**Sec. 70421. Permanent renewal and enhancement of opportunity zones.**
This makes the Opportunity Zone program permanent with decennial redesignations every 10 years beginning July 1, 2026, tightens low-income community eligibility (70% of area median income, or 20% poverty rate plus 125% of median income), and repeals the contiguous-tract rule and the Puerto Rico designation provision (effective December 31, 2026). For investments after December 31, 2026, deferred gains are included in income after 5 years (rather than the prior structure), with a 10% basis step-up at 5 years—or 30% for investments in "qualified rural opportunity funds" holding 90%+ of assets in rural-zone property (rural defined as areas outside cities/towns over 50,000 population)—and the 10-year full exclusion of appreciation is extended to 30 years. New information reporting requirements (IRC §§6039K, 6039L) require qualified opportunity funds to file detailed annual returns on assets, investments, census tracts, residential units, and employees, with penalties of $500/day (capped at $10,000, or $50,000 for funds with gross assets over $10 million; doubled for intentional disregard). The IRS receives $15 million (available through September 30, 2028) to produce annual public reports on fund activity, investments by NAICS code, employment, housing units, and—starting in year 6—economic outcomes like job creation and poverty reduction.

**Sec. 70422. Permanent enhancement of low-income housing tax credit.**
This makes permanent the 12.5% increase in the state housing credit ceiling (9% credits) at a per-resident allocation factor of 1.12 for calendar years after 2025. It also modifies the "50% test" for tax-exempt bond financing: buildings qualify for 4% credits if at least 50% of aggregate basis (building plus land) is bond-financed, or if at least 25% is bond-financed where bonds issued after December 31, 2025 finance at least 5% of aggregate basis. Effective for buildings placed in service in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70423. Permanent extension of new markets tax credit.**
This makes the New Markets Tax Credit (IRC §45D) allocation authority permanent at $5 billion per calendar year (the prior annual amount) for all years after 2019, rather than expiring after 2025. Unused allocation authority may be carried forward for up to 5 calendar years, with pre-2026 excess treated as occurring in 2025. Effective for calendar years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70424. Permanent and expanded reinstatement of partial deduction for charitable contributions of individuals who do not elect to itemize.**
This makes permanent and expands the non-itemizer charitable deduction, raising the limit from $300 ($600 for joint filers) to $1,000 ($2,000 for joint filers). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70425. 0.5 percent floor on deduction of contributions made by individuals.**
This imposes a 0.5%-of-contribution-base floor on itemized charitable deductions for individuals—only contributions exceeding 0.5% of adjusted gross income (contribution base) are deductible, applied in a specified ordering across contribution types (capital gain property to public charities first, then cash, etc.). Amounts disallowed by the floor carry forward only from years in which the taxpayer exceeds the applicable percentage limitations, and the floor also applies to the non-itemizer deduction. The 60%-of-AGI limit for cash contributions is retained but coordinated with the new floor. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70426. 1-percent floor on deduction of charitable contributions made by corporations.**
This imposes a 1%-of-taxable-income floor on corporate charitable deductions—only contributions exceeding 1% of taxable income are deductible, within the existing 10%-of-taxable-income cap. Contributions disallowed by the 10% cap carry forward up to 5 years on a first-in, first-out basis, with special rules ensuring the floor doesn't create or increase net operating loss carryovers. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70427. Permanent increase in limitation on cover over of tax on distilled spirits.**
This permanently increases the per-proof-gallon cover-over amount of federal excise tax on distilled spirits that is transferred to Puerto Rico and the Virgin Islands from $10.50 to $13.25. Effective for distilled spirits brought into the United States after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70428. Nonprofit community development activities in remote native villages.**
This provides that fisheries-related activities (harvesting, processing, transportation, sales, and marketing of fish and fish products) in the Bering Sea and Aleutian Islands carried on by the six Western Alaska Community Development Quota entities identified in section 305(i)(1)(D) of the Magnuson-Stevens Act are treated as related to their tax-exempt purposes under IRC §501(a). Additionally, if a wholly owned subsidiary transfers such a fisheries trade or business to the parent entity within 18 months of enactment, no gain is recognized on the transfer and all income from the transferred business is tax-exempt. Effective on enactment and remains effective during the existence of the western Alaska community development quota program.

**Sec. 70429. Adjustment of charitable deduction for certain expenses incurred in support of Native Alaskan subsistence whaling.**
This raises from $10,000 to $50,000 the cap on the charitable deduction for expenses incurred in support of qualified Native Alaskan subsistence whaling captains (under IRC §170(n)). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70430. Exception to percentage of completion method of accounting for certain residential construction contracts.**
This expands the exception from the percentage-of-completion accounting method to cover all "residential construction contracts" (not just smaller "home construction contracts"), allowing contractors to use the completed-contract method if the contract is completed within a 3-year period beginning when the contract is entered into (a 2-year test applies to home construction contracts). It also repeals the $10 million average annual gross receipts limitation and conformingly updates the alternative minimum tax rules. Effective for contracts entered into in taxable years beginning after enactment.

## Subchapter D — Investments in Small Business and Rural America

**Sec. 70431. Expansion of qualified small business stock gain exclusion.**
This expands the §1202 qualified small business stock (QSBS) exclusion with a tiered holding schedule for stock acquired after enactment: 50% of gain excluded for stock held at least 3 years, 75% for 4 years, and 100% for 5 years or more (stock acquired on or before enactment retains the prior 5-year/50%-or-100% rules). The per-issuer exclusion cap rises from $10 million to $15 million (inflation-adjusted after 2026) for post-enactment stock, and the aggregate gross assets limit for qualifying corporations rises from $50 million to $75 million (inflation-adjusted after 2026) for stock issued after enactment. The 100% exclusion for pre-2010 stock remains excluded from AMT preference; effective for taxable years beginning after enactment (asset-limit change applies to stock issued after enactment).

**Sec. 70432. Repeal of revision to de minimis rules for third party network transactions.**
This reinstates the pre-American Rescue Plan Act de minimis threshold for Form 1099-K reporting by third-party settlement organizations (e.g., PayPal, Venmo): reporting is required only if a payee has over $20,000 in transactions and more than 200 transactions in a calendar year, reversing the $600 threshold. The de minimis rule is also applied to backup withholding, with an exception if the payee's prior-year transactions were reportable. The reporting change applies retroactively as if included in the American Rescue Plan Act; the backup withholding provision applies to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70433. Increase in threshold for requiring information reporting with respect to certain payees.**
This raises the Form 1099 information reporting threshold (IRC §6041) from $600 to $2,000 for payments made in the course of a trade or business, with inflation adjustments after 2026 rounded to the nearest $100. The new threshold also applies to payments to attorneys (§6041A) and to backup withholding rules (§3406), and changes the measurement period from taxable year to calendar year. Effective for payments made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70434. Treatment of certain qualified sound recording productions.**
This extends §181 expensing to "qualified sound recording productions"—sound recordings produced and recorded in the United States—allowing producers to elect to expense up to $150,000 of aggregate annual production costs rather than amortizing them. Sound recordings are also made eligible for 100% bonus depreciation under §168(k), treated as placed in service at initial release or broadcast. The section heading is changed to "Treatment of certain qualified productions," and the provisions apply to productions commencing in taxable years ending after enactment.

**Sec. 70435. Exclusion of interest on loans secured by rural or agricultural real property.**
This creates a new exclusion (IRC §139L) allowing qualified lenders—FDIC-insured banks and savings associations, state/federally regulated insurance companies, U.S.-based subsidiaries of bank or insurance holding companies, and Farm Credit System instrumentalities—to exclude 25% of interest income on qualified real estate loans secured by rural or agricultural real estate. Qualifying property includes land substantially used for agricultural production, fishing or seafood processing businesses, and aquaculture facilities (hatcheries, rearing ponds, raceways, pens, incubators) located in a U.S. state or possession. Loans must be made after enactment to borrowers other than specified foreign entities, with refinancings of pre-enactment loans excluded; coordination rules under §265 prevent double benefits. Effective for taxable years ending after enactment.

**Sec. 70436. Reduction of transfer and manufacturing taxes for certain devices.**
This eliminates the $200 federal transfer tax (§5811) and making tax (§5821) on firearms other than machineguns and destructive devices, effectively removing the National Firearms Act tax on silencers, short-barreled rifles, short-barreled shotguns, and "any other weapons." The $200 tax remains for machineguns and destructive devices, and a conforming amendment to §4182 deems non-NFA firearms to have had the transfer tax paid. Effective for calendar quarters beginning more than 90 days after enactment.

**Sec. 70437. Treatment of capital gains from the sale of certain farmland property.**
This creates a new provision (IRC §1062) allowing sellers of "qualified farmland property" to elect to pay the net income tax attributable to the sale in 4 equal annual installments rather than all at once. Qualifying property must have been used or leased for farming during substantially all of the 10-year period before the sale and be subject to a legally enforceable covenant restricting non-farming use for 10 years after the sale; the buyer must be a "qualified farmer" actively engaged in farming under the Food Security Act of 1986. Installments accelerate upon death (individuals), liquidation/substantial asset sale (C corporations, trusts, estates), or failure to timely pay, and deficiencies are prorated across installments (unless due to negligence, intentional disregard, or fraud). Effective for sales or exchanges in taxable years beginning after enactment.

**Sec. 70438. Extension of rules for treatment of certain disaster-related personal casualty losses.**
This extends the temporary rules from the Taxpayer Certainty and Disaster Tax Relief Act of 2020 (which allowed casualty loss deductions exceeding $500 net of insurance, and penalty-free retirement account withdrawals, for federally declared disasters) by substituting the date of enactment of this Act for the original December 27, 2020 enactment date—thereby covering disasters declared through the new enactment date.

**Sec. 70439. Restoration of taxable REIT subsidiary asset test.**
This restores the taxable REIT subsidiary asset test in IRC §856(c)(4)(B)(ii), increasing the limit on securities of taxable REIT subsidiaries from 20% to 25% of a REIT's total assets. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025. [end of text]


